    46	
    47	## Definition
    48	
    49	Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],
    50	[[def-real-numbers]]) with its order and absolute value ([[def-real-order]]), and
    51	$\mathbb{N}$ is the set of natural numbers with its order ([[def-natural-numbers]],
    52	[[def-nat-order]]).
    53	
    54	A **sequence of reals** is a function $x : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$. We write
    55	$x_k$ for the value $x(k)$, call it the **$k$-th term**, and write $(x_k)$, or
    56	$(x_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$, for the function itself. The **range** of $(x_k)$ is
    57	the subset $\{\, x_k : k \in \mathbb{N} \,\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$.
    58	
    59	Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals and let $P$ be a property of indices.
    60	
    61	- $(x_k)$ is **bounded** if there is $M \in \mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for
    62	  every $k \in \mathbb{N}$; it is **unbounded** otherwise.
    63	- $P$ holds **eventually** if there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $P(k)$ holds
    64	  for every $k \ge K$.
    65	- $P$ holds **frequently** if for every $K \in \mathbb{N}$ there is some
    66	  $k \ge K$ for which $P(k)$ holds.
    67	- For $K \in \mathbb{N}$, the **$K$-th tail** of $(x_k)$ is the sequence
    68	  $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j + K}$ for $j \in \mathbb{N}$; it is
    69	  again a sequence of reals.
    70	- A function $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is **strictly increasing** if
    71	  $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$. For such an $n$, the **subsequence** of $(x_k)$
    72	  along $n$ is the composite $x \circ n$, written $(x_{n_k})_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$;
    73	  it is again a sequence of reals.
    74	
    75	**Convergence and Cauchyness are not defined here.** They are already fixed, for
    76	sequences of reals, by the published [[def-real-limit]]: $(x_k)$ *converges* to
    77	$x \in \mathbb{R}$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is
    78	$K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$, and $(x_k)$
    79	is *Cauchy* when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$
    80	with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$. This page builds the
    81	toolkit for those two notions and does not restate them. A sequence
    82	**converges** if it converges to some real, and **diverges** if it does not.
    83	
    84	## Remarks
    85	
    86	- **Identification of $\mathbb{Q}$ with its image.** The map $q \mapsto \hat q$
    87	  is an embedding of ordered fields ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), and as is
    88	  standard we write $q$ for $\hat q$, so that a rational may be compared with a
    89	  real without further comment. [[def-real-limit]] is stated with the hat; every
    90	  rational $\varepsilon$ occurring on this page is its image under this
    91	  embedding.
    92	
    93	- **Rational and real $\varepsilon$ agree.** [[def-real-limit]] tests convergence
    94	  against every *rational* $\varepsilon > 0$, and its own remark records that
    95	  this loses nothing. Spelled out: suppose that for every rational
    96	  $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all
    97	  $k \ge K$, and let $\eta > 0$ be an arbitrary *real*. Since $0 < \eta$,
    98	  density of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]) supplies a
    99	  rational $\varepsilon$ with $0 < \varepsilon < \eta$, and the index $K$
   100	  belonging to that $\varepsilon$ satisfies $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon < \eta$ for
   101	  all $k \ge K$. The converse implication is immediate, since every positive
   102	  rational is a positive real. So the two formulations define the same relation,
   103	  and the same two lines apply verbatim to the Cauchy condition and to any
   104	  condition of the shape "for every $\varepsilon > 0$, eventually
   105	  $\cdots < \varepsilon$". Proofs on this page therefore run the test with a real
   106	  $\varepsilon$ wherever that is more convenient, and say so by citing this
   107	  remark; nothing is smuggled in.
   108	
   109	- **Constant sequences converge.** For $c \in \mathbb{R}$ the constant sequence
   110	  $x_k := c$ converges to $c$, because $|x_k - c| = |0| = 0 < \varepsilon$ for
   111	  every $\varepsilon > 0$ and every $k$; it is bounded by $|c|$.
   112	
   113	- **Eventually and frequently are dual.** $P$ holds frequently exactly when
   114	  $\lnot P$ does not hold eventually, and $P$ holds eventually exactly when
   115	  $\lnot P$ does not hold frequently. So the two quantifier patterns are
   116	  negations of one another applied to the complementary property, and there is no
   117	  third pattern hiding between them.
   118	
   119	- **A sequence is not its range.** $(x_k)$ is a function, not a set, and the
   120	  range does not determine the function: the sequence $x_0 = 0$, $x_k = 1$ for
   121	  $k \ge 1$ and the sequence $y_0 = y_1 = 0$, $y_k = 1$ for $k \ge 2$ have the
   122	  same range $\{0, 1\}$, yet they differ at $k = 1$, so they are different
   123	  sequences. Order and repetition are part of the data and the range forgets
   124	  both. Boundedness, on the other hand, depends only on the range.
   125	
   126	- **Indexing.** Sequences here start at $k = 0$ because $\mathbb{N}$ contains
   127	  $0$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]). Texts that index from $1$ describe the same
   128	  objects up to a shift, and the shift changes nothing about convergence, by
   129	  [[lem-limit-of-tail]].
