    41	
    42	## Statement
    43	
    44	Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]), let $x \in \mathbb{R}$,
    45	and for $K \in \mathbb{N}$ let $x^{(K)}$ denote the $K$-th tail, $x^{(K)}_j =
    46	x_{j+K}$. The following are equivalent ([[def-real-limit]]):
    47	
    48	1. $(x_k)$ converges to $x$;
    49	2. every tail $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$;
    50	3. some tail $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$.
    51	
    52	The same three statements with "converges to $x$" replaced by "is Cauchy" are
    53	likewise equivalent. Consequently, if two sequences agree from some index on,
    54	then either both converge to $x$ or neither does, and either both are Cauchy or
    55	neither is.
    56	
    57	## Facts & Assumptions
    58	
    59	**Given:** A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals, a real $x$, and for each $K \in \mathbb{N}$ the $K$-th tail $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j+K}$ ([[def-sequence]]).
    60	
    61	[L1] Convergence and the Cauchy condition: $(z_k)$ converges to $z$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge N$, and $(z_k)$ is Cauchy when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge N$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
    62	
    63	[L2] Index arithmetic, first half: $j \ge N$ implies $j + K \ge N + K$, and $N + K \ge N$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-order-add-compatible]]).
    64	
    65	[L3] Index arithmetic, second half: every $k \ge N + K$ has the form $k = j + K$ with $j \ge N$. Indeed $N + K \le k$ gives $i$ with $(N+K) + i = k$; put $j := N + i$, so that $j \ge N$ and $j + K = (N+i) + K = N + (i + K) = N + (K + i) = (N+K) + i = k$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).
    66	
    67	[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is reflexive and transitive, so $k \ge N + K \ge N$ gives $k \ge N$; and $j + 0 = j$ for every $j$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]]).
    68	
    69	## Proof
    70	
    71	**Proof technique:** direct.
    72	
    73	1.1 (1) implies (2). Assume $(x_k)$ converges to $x$, let $K \in \mathbb{N}$ be arbitrary and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge N$. For every $j \ge N$ we have $j + K \ge N + K \ge N$, hence $|x^{(K)}_j - x| = |x_{j+K} - x| < \varepsilon$; so $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$. [assume-hyp, L1, L2, L4]
    74	
    75	1.2 (2) implies (3), in both families. The $0$-th tail satisfies $x^{(0)}_j = x_{j+0} = x_j$, so $(x_k)$ is itself one of its own tails; statement (2) quantifies over all tails, so applying it to this one already yields (3). [given, L4]
    76	
    77	1.3 (3) implies (1). Assume $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$ for some fixed $K$, and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x^{(K)}_j - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $j \ge N$. Every $k \ge N + K$ is of the form $j + K$ with $j \ge N$, so $|x_k - x| = |x^{(K)}_j - x| < \varepsilon$; taking $N + K$ as the threshold shows $(x_k)$ converges to $x$. [assume-hyp, L1, L3]
    78	
    79	1.4 The Cauchy version of (1) implies (2). Assume $(x_k)$ is Cauchy, fix $K$ and a rational $\varepsilon > 0$, and take $N$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge N$. For $i, j \ge N$ both $i + K \ge N$ and $j + K \ge N$, so $|x^{(K)}_i - x^{(K)}_j| = |x_{i+K} - x_{j+K}| < \varepsilon$; so $x^{(K)}$ is Cauchy. [assume-hyp, L1, L2, L4]
    80	
    81	1.5 The Cauchy version of (3) implies (1). Assume $x^{(K)}$ is Cauchy and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x^{(K)}_i - x^{(K)}_j| < \varepsilon$ for all $i, j \ge N$. Any $k, l \ge N + K$ are of the form $i + K$ and $j + K$ with $i, j \ge N$, so $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$; so $(x_k)$ is Cauchy. [assume-hyp, L1, L3]
    82	
    83	2.1 In each of the two families the cycle (1) implies (2) implies (3) implies (1) is closed, so within each family the three statements are equivalent. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5]
    84	
    85	3.1 If $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ agree from index $K$ on, then $x^{(K)} = y^{(K)}$ as functions, so by the established equivalence $(x_k)$ converges to $x$ exactly when $y^{(K)}$ does, exactly when $(y_k)$ does, and the same chain applies to the Cauchy condition. [step 2.1, given] ∎
    86	
    87	## Remarks
    88	
    89	- This is the lemma that makes the word "eventually" usable. Once it is known that finitely many initial terms are irrelevant, a hypothesis of the form "$P(k)$ for all $k$" can everywhere be weakened to "$P(k)$ eventually" ([[def-sequence]]) at the cost of passing to a tail.
    90	
    91	- It also settles the bookkeeping question of where the indexing starts. A text that writes $(x_k)_{k \ge 1}$ is describing the first tail of a sequence in the sense used here, and the two have the same limits and the same Cauchy status.
    92	
    93	- Boundedness, by contrast, is *not* a tail property in the same trivial way: a tail of a bounded sequence is bounded, and a sequence with a bounded tail is bounded only because the finitely many omitted terms can be absorbed into the bound, which is exactly the argument of [[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]].
