    41	
    42	## Statement
    43	
    44	Let $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ be sequences of reals ([[def-sequence]]). If $(x_k)$
    45	converges to $0$ ([[def-real-limit]]) and $(y_k)$ is bounded, then the product
    46	sequence $(x_k y_k)$ converges to $0$.
    47	
    48	No assumption is made that $(y_k)$ converges. Boundedness alone suffices, and
    49	that is why this lemma is stated on its own rather than folded into the product
    50	rule for limits.
    51	
    52	## Facts & Assumptions
    53	
    54	**Given:** Sequences $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ of reals with $(x_k)$ converging to $0$, and a real $M$ with $|y_k| \le M$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
    55	
    56	[L1] Convergence, quantified over rational $\varepsilon > 0$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
    57	
    58	[L2] Absolute value: $|uv| = |u|\,|v|$, $|u| \ge 0$, and $|u - 0| = |u|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
    59	
    60	[L3] Products of inequalities: $0 \le a \le b$ and $0 \le c \le d$ give $ac \le bd$; and for $c > 0$, $a < b$ gives $ac < bc$ ([[prop-of-multiply-inequalities]], [[lem-of-sign-rules]]).
    61	
    62	[L4] Order arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: $0 < 1$, adding a constant preserves the strict order, and $\le$ and $<$ compose transitively ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
    63	
    64	[L5] Inverses: if $c > 0$ then $c^{-1} > 0$, so $\varepsilon/c > 0$ whenever $\varepsilon > 0$ and $c > 0$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]], [[lem-of-sign-rules]]).
    65	
    66	[L6] Real versus rational $\varepsilon$: for every real $\eta > 0$ there is a rational $\varepsilon$ with $0 < \varepsilon < \eta$, by density ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]) or by the Archimedean property ([[thm-of-archimedean]]) applied to $1/\eta$; consequently the convergence test of [[def-real-limit]] may equivalently be run with real $\varepsilon > 0$ ([[def-sequence]]).
    67	
    68	## Proof
    69	
    70	**Proof technique:** direct.
    71	
    72	1.1 Since $|y_0| \le M$ and $|y_0| \ge 0$, transitivity gives $M \ge 0$; put $C := M + 1$, so that $C > 0$ and $|y_k| \le M \le C$ for every $k$. [L2, L4]
    73	
    74	2.1 Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be an arbitrary real and put $\eta := \varepsilon/C$, which is a real $> 0$ and satisfies $\eta C = \varepsilon$. [step 1.1, L5]
    75	
    76	3.1 Since $(x_k)$ converges to $0$, there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k| = |x_k - 0| < \eta$ for every $k \ge K$. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L6]
    77	
    78	4.1 For every $k \ge K$ we get $|x_k y_k - 0| = |x_k|\,|y_k| \le |x_k| \, C < \eta \, C = \varepsilon$, the first inequality from $0 \le |x_k| \le |x_k|$ and $0 \le |y_k| \le C$, the second from $|x_k| < \eta$ and $C > 0$. [step 1.1, step 3.1, L2, L3, L4]
    79	
    80	5.1 Since the real $\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, $(x_k y_k)$ converges to $0$. [step 4.1, L1, L6] ∎
    81	
    82	## Remarks
    83	
    84	- The hypothesis on $(y_k)$ looks weaker if it is stated as "eventually bounded", but it is not: a sequence bounded from some index $K$ on is bounded outright. If $|y_k| \le M$ for every $k \ge K$, then $M' := \max\{\, |y_0|, \dots, |y_{K-1}|, M \,\}$ exists, because a nonempty finite list of reals has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]), and $|y_k| \le M'$ for every $k$. That is the same absorption of finitely many initial terms used in [[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]].
    85	
    86	- Boundedness of $(y_k)$ is essential. Take $x_k := 1/(k+1)$, which is null (that sequence is shown to converge to $0$ in [[fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality]]), and $y_k := (k+1)^2$. Their product is $x_k y_k = k + 1$, which is unbounded by the Archimedean property ([[thm-of-archimedean]]) and hence does not converge at all, since a convergent sequence is bounded ([[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]]); in particular it is not null. The lemma is therefore sharp in the sense that the bounded factor may not be replaced by an arbitrary one.
    87	
    88	- The lemma is the workhorse of the product rule: the identity $x_k y_k - xy = x_k(y_k - y) + y(x_k - x)$ exhibits the error as a sum of two products of a null sequence with a bounded one, and boundedness of $(x_k)$ comes from [[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]].

