    34	
    35	This page fixes a handful of conventions. None of them is deep, and each is the
    36	kind of thing that silently causes trouble when it is left implicit.
    37	
    38	**Indexing from $0$ or from $1$ makes no difference to convergence.** Sequences
    39	here are functions on $\mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]]), and $\mathbb{N}$ contains
    40	$0$, so the first term is $x_0$. A text that writes $(x_k)_{k \ge 1}$ is
    41	describing the first tail of such a function. By [[lem-limit-of-tail]] a sequence
    42	and any of its tails converge to exactly the same limits and are Cauchy together,
    43	so every statement on this page about convergence, about limits and about the
    44	Cauchy condition reads the same under a shift of the indexing. That is a claim
    45	about those statements and not about every sentence here: the starting index is
    46	genuinely visible wherever an index is named, as in the base case $n_0 \ge 0$ of
    47	[[lem-index-map-grows]], in the $0$-th tail used in the proof of
    48	[[lem-limit-of-tail]], and in the recursion $s_0 = 1$ that builds the witness of
    49	[[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]]. In a formula it shows up as the shift in
    50	$1/(k+1)$, written that way here purely so that no term is undefined at $k = 0$.
    51	
    52	**"Eventually" is the only weakened quantifier pattern used for hypotheses.** A
    53	property holds eventually when it holds for all indices from some point on, and
    54	frequently when, for every index $K$, it holds at some index $k \ge K$
    55	([[def-sequence]]). The two are negations of each other applied to the
    56	complementary property: $P$ holds frequently exactly when $\lnot P$ does not hold
    57	eventually. "Frequently" is *defined* in that cofinal form, and the cofinal form
    58	is what every use of the word here means, because it is the literal negation of
    59	"eventually" and needs nothing further. The familiar reading "at infinitely many
    60	indices" is equivalent to it, and the equivalence is available in this library
    61	rather than merely plausible: finiteness is defined ([[def-countable]]),
    62	[[lem-subset-of-countable]] proves that a subset of $\mathbb{N}$ is finite when
    63	it is bounded above and countably infinite when it is not, and
    64	[[lem-pigeonhole]] proves that $\mathbb{N}$ is equinumerous with no natural
    65	number, so those two cases exclude one another. A set of indices is therefore
    66	cofinal exactly when it is infinite. The cofinal form is nevertheless the one
    67	taken as the definition here, because it is the literal negation of "eventually"
    68	and calls on no counting theory at all. Because of [[lem-limit-of-tail]], a
    69	hypothesis of the form "for all $k$"
    70	whose conclusion concerns only convergence may be weakened to "eventually" at no
    71	cost, and two of the three hypotheses of that shape on this page are stated in
    72	the weaker form: the comparison $x_k \le y_k$ of [[lem-limit-preserves-order]]
    73	and the bracketing $a_k \le x_k \le b_k$ of [[thm-squeeze]]. The third is
    74	deliberately left at every index, the strict comparison $x_k < y_k$ of
    75	[[fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality]]: that claim is false, and stating its
    76	hypothesis at every index makes it as strong as it can be, so that the refutation
    77	defeats the strongest form rather than a weakened one. The remaining hypotheses
    78	are stated at every index because they are not of that shape. Some are needed at
    79	every index for the statement to typecheck at all, such as $y_k \ne 0$ in
    80	[[thm-algebra-of-limits]] and $x_k > 0$ in [[lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges]],
    81	where a missing index would leave $1/y_k$ or $1/x_k$ undefined; and the bound
    82	$|y_k| \le M$ of [[lem-null-times-bounded]] is not weakened because, as recorded
    83	there, an eventual bound is already a bound. "Frequently" is defined here for
    84	completeness and is used in no hypothesis on this page.
    85	
    86	**$\lim$ is written only after uniqueness is known.** The notation $\lim_k x_k$
    87	presupposes that at most one real can be the limit, which is
    88	[[lem-limit-unique]]. Before that lemma, the correct phrasing is "$(x_k)$
    89	converges to $x$", a relation between a sequence and a real, not a function of
    90	the sequence. The same discipline is applied to $\sup$ and $\inf$ elsewhere in
    91	the library ([[rem-sup-conventions]]): a notation is introduced only once the
    92	object it names has been shown to exist and to be unique.
    93	
    94	**Convergence is tested against rational $\varepsilon$.** The published
    95	[[def-real-limit]] quantifies over rational $\varepsilon > 0$ rather than real
    96	$\varepsilon > 0$. That is a deliberate feature of the construction of
    97	$\mathbb{R}$, where the rationals are available before the reals are complete,
    98	and it loses nothing: below any real $\eta > 0$ lies a positive rational
    99	([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), so the two formulations define the same relation. The
   100	argument is written out once, in the remarks of [[def-sequence]], and proofs on
   101	this page cite it whenever a real $\varepsilon$ is more convenient than a
   102	rational one. Rationals are identified with their images in $\mathbb{R}$ under
   103	the ordered-field embedding, again as recorded in [[def-sequence]].
   104	
   105	**A sequence is not its range, but boundedness only sees the range.** $(x_k)$ is
   106	a function; the set $\{x_k : k \in \mathbb{N}\}$ forgets order and multiplicity.
   107	The sequence is bounded, in the sense of [[def-sequence]], exactly when its range
   108	is a bounded subset of $\mathbb{R}$ in the sense of [[def-bounded-set]]. Both
   109	directions rest on the equivalence $|x| \le M \iff -M \le x \le M$: if
   110	$|x| \le M$ then $x \le |x| \le M$ and $-M \le -|x| \le x$, using
   111	$-|x| \le x \le |x|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]) together with the fact that
   112	$a \le b$ and $-b \le -a$ are the same assertion, both saying that $b - a$ is
   113	positive or zero ([[def-ordered-field]]); conversely, if $-M \le x \le M$ then
   114	$|x|$, being $x$ or $-x$ by the definition of the absolute value
   115	([[def-real-order]], [[def-abs-value]]), is $\le M$ either way. Given a
   116	bound $M$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for all $k$, the range is bounded below by $-M$ and
   117	above by $M$. Conversely, given $\ell \le x_k \le u$ for all $k$, put
   118	$M := \max\{|\ell|, |u|\}$, which exists because a nonempty finite list of reals
   119	has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]); then
   120	$x_k \le u \le |u| \le M$ and $-M \le -|\ell| \le \ell \le x_k$, so
   121	$-M \le x_k \le M$ and hence $|x_k| \le M$ for every $k$, by transitivity of
   122	$\le$ ([[def-ordered-field]]).
   123	
   124	Boundedness is not alone in this. Any property of $(x_k)$ that is defined purely
   125	from the set of values is settled by the range for the same trivial reason:
   126	bounded above, bounded below, and the supremum or infimum of the range when it
   127	exists ([[def-bounded-set]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]) are read off the
   128	range by definition, so permuting or repeating terms cannot change them.
   129	**Convergence is not of that kind**, and that is the contrast worth drawing: the
   130	sequence $z_0 = -1$ with $z_k = 1$ for $k \ge 1$ and the alternating sequence of
   131	[[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]] have the *same* range $\{-1, 1\}$, yet the
   132	first converges to $1$, being constant from index $1$ on ([[def-sequence]],
   133	[[lem-limit-of-tail]]), while the second does not converge at all. So knowing the
   134	range settles boundedness and does not settle convergence. That is the whole of
   135	what is claimed here; each other property of a sequence has to be looked at on
   136	its own, and this page does not survey them.
   137	
   138	**"Diverges" means "does not converge".** Divergence is the plain negation of
   139	convergence, so an oscillating bounded sequence diverges. Divergence *to*
   140	$+\infty$ or $-\infty$ ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]) is a strictly stronger
   141	and entirely separate condition, and the arrow in $x_k \to +\infty$ is an
   142	abbreviation, not an equation: this library never writes $\lim_k x_k = +\infty$,
   143	because $+\infty$ is not a real number.
   144	
   145	**What is deliberately absent from this page.** Monotone sequences, the monotone
   146	convergence theorem, the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem and the completeness of
   147	$\mathbb{R}$ in the Cauchy sense are none of them treated here. They are the
   148	subject of the next page of this track, *Monotone Sequences,
   149	Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, which comes later in the reading
   150	order; nothing on this page may be taken to have proved any of
   151	them. The last of the four is the one exception worth stating: for the
   152	$\mathbb{R}$ this page works in, every Cauchy sequence of reals converges to a
   153	real is already proved elsewhere in the library, by
   154	[[thm-reals-cauchy-complete]] on the Cauchy-construction page, and
   155	[[lem-convergent-implies-cauchy]] records where that leaves the two directions.
   156	The monotone convergence theorem and the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem have no such
   157	exception: neither is proved anywhere in this library as things stand.
   158	**How far this page is really a page about $\mathbb{R}$.** It assumes only that
   159	$\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field, and it uses completeness solely through
   160	the Archimedean property. It is tempting to conclude that every result here holds
   161	verbatim for sequences of rationals, and the earlier version of this remark said
   162	exactly that. The claim needs splitting in two, because it is true of the
   163	statements and not literally true of the proofs.
   164	
   165	The statements do transfer. $\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field
   166	([[thm-rat-ordered-field]]) and is Archimedean ([[lem-rat-archimedean]]), and the
   167	absolute value, the triangle inequality and the order arithmetic these proofs run
   168	on are established for an arbitrary ordered field, not for $\mathbb{R}$ in
   169	particular ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[lem-of-triangle-inequality]],
   170	[[def-ordered-field]]).
   171	
   172	The proofs do not transfer by citation, and two places are why.
   173	[[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]] absorbs the first finitely many terms using
   174	[[lem-finite-set-has-max]], which is stated for finite lists of **reals**; its
   175	proof is an induction that runs in any totally ordered field, but as stated it
   176	does not apply to $\mathbb{Q}$. And [[thm-of-archimedean]] is stated for
   177	**complete** ordered fields, so it says nothing about $\mathbb{Q}$; the
   178	$\mathbb{Q}$ counterpart is the separately proved [[lem-rat-archimedean]]. A
   179	third, more basic point: this library defines convergence and the Cauchy
   180	condition only for sequences of reals ([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]), so
   181	the rational statements are not formulated anywhere here in the first place.
   182	
   183	What is therefore claimed, and all that is claimed, is this: rerun any argument on
   184	this page over $\mathbb{Q}$, replacing the maximum lemma by the same induction in
   185	$\mathbb{Q}$ and [[thm-of-archimedean]] by [[lem-rat-archimedean]], and it goes
   186	through unchanged. That is a statement about the arguments, not a licence to cite
   187	the items above with $\mathbb{Q}$ in place of $\mathbb{R}$.

