items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:54:[L2] Triangle inequality: $|a + b| \le |a| + |b|$ in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:56:[L3] Absolute value: $|-u| = |u|$ for every real $u$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:58:[L4] Halving a positive rational: if $\varepsilon$ is a rational $> 0$ then $\varepsilon/2$ is again a rational, it is $> 0$, and $\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$. In detail, $\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-field]]), so $1 > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]) and $2 := 1 + 1 > 0$ because the positives are closed under addition ([[def-ordered-field]]); hence $2 \ne 0$ is invertible with $2^{-1} > 0$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]), the product $\varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1}$ of two positives is positive ([[lem-of-sign-rules]]), and $\varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} + \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} = \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} \cdot 2 = \varepsilon$ by the field axioms ([[def-field]]). The embedding of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$ preserves the order ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), so these facts hold verbatim for the images, under the identification recorded in [[def-sequence]].
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:60:[L5] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is total and transitive, so a single threshold serves for both indices ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:62:[L6] Order arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: adding two strict inequalities, $a < b$ and $c < d$ give $a + c < b + d$ ([[lem-of-add-order]]); and, since $u \le v$ means $u < v$ or $u = v$, the mixed form $u \le v < w \Rightarrow u < w$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:61:[L1] Convergence and the Cauchy condition: $(z_k)$ converges to $z$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge N$, and $(z_k)$ is Cauchy when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge N$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:63:[L2] Index arithmetic, first half: $j \ge N$ implies $j + K \ge N + K$, and $N + K \ge N$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-order-add-compatible]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:65:[L3] Index arithmetic, second half: every $k \ge N + K$ has the form $k = j + K$ with $j \ge N$. Indeed $N + K \le k$ gives $i$ with $(N+K) + i = k$; put $j := N + i$, so that $j \ge N$ and $j + K = (N+i) + K = N + (i + K) = N + (K + i) = (N+K) + i = k$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:67:[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is reflexive and transitive, so $k \ge N + K \ge N$ gives $k \ge N$; and $j + 0 = j$ for every $j$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]]).

