FILE items/cor-of-one-positive.md
33:## Statement
34:
35:In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity
36:satisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \in P$.
37:
38:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/def-complete-ordered-field.md
29:## Definition
30:
31:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq F$.
32:
33:- $u \in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$; $S$ is
34:  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.
35:- $u \in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$
36:  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.
37:
38:$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the
39:**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty
40:$S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.
41:
42:## Remarks
FILE items/def-field.md
35:## Definition
36:
37:A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and
38:$\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \ne 1$ of $F$,
39:satisfying:
40:
41:- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is
42:  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an
43:  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.
44:- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and
45:  $x \cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \in F$; moreover $(F \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$ is an
46:  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse
47:  $x^{-1}$ with $x \cdot x^{-1} = 1$.
48:- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \cdot (y + z) = x\cdot y + x\cdot z$.
49:
50:We write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \ne 0$), and
51:abbreviate $x \cdot y$ as $xy$.
52:
53:## Remarks
FILE items/def-max-min.md
38:## Definition
39:
40:Let $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and $m \in \mathbb{R}$.
41:
42:- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \in S$ and $s \le m$
43:  for every $s \in S$.
44:- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \in S$ and $m \le s$
45:  for every $s \in S$.
46:
47:A set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then
48:$m_1 \in S$ gives $m_1 \le m_2$ and $m_2 \in S$ gives $m_2 \le m_1$, so
49:$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy
50:axiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
51:The same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\max S$ and $\min S$.
52:
53:## Remarks
FILE items/def-nat-addition.md
31:## Definition
32:
33:**Addition** $+ : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is defined by
34:recursion on the second argument: for each fixed $m \in \mathbb{N}$,
35:
36:$$m + 0 = m, \qquad m + \sigma(n) = \sigma(m + n).$$
37:
38:## Remarks
FILE items/def-nat-order.md
36:## Definition
37:
38:For $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$, define the **order** by
39:
40:$$m \le n \iff \exists k \in \mathbb{N}\ (m + k = n),$$
41:
42:and the **strict order** by $m < n \iff (m \le n \text{ and } m \neq n)$, using
43:addition ([[def-nat-addition]]).
44:
45:## Remarks
FILE items/def-ordered-field.md
29:## Definition
30:
31:An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset
32:$P \subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),
33:satisfying:
34:
35:- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \in F$, exactly one of the following holds:
36:  $x \in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \in P$.
37:- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \in P$ then $x + y \in P$ and $x \cdot y \in P$.
38:
39:We write $x > 0$ for $x \in P$ (read "$x$ is positive"), and define the order by
40:
41:$$a < b \;:\Longleftrightarrow\; b - a \in P, \qquad a \le b \;:\Longleftrightarrow\; a < b \text{ or } a = b.$$
42:
43:Thus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \ge b$ means $b \le a$. An element $x$ with
44:$-x \in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.
45:
46:## Remarks
FILE items/def-real-limit.md
25:## Definition
26:
27:A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals **converges** to $x \in \mathbb{R}$ when for
28:every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with
29:$|x_k - x| < \hat\varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$. It is **Cauchy** when for
30:every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with
31:$|x_k - x_l| < \hat\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$.
32:
33:## Remarks
FILE items/lem-finite-set-has-max.md
38:## Statement
39:
40:For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \dots, a_n \in \mathbb{R}$, the
41:set $\{a_0, a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).
42:
43:What is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.
44:
45:The usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$ has a
46:maximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets
47:of $\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$. That
48:identification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page
49:has no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not
50:merely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two
51:descriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is
52:recorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this
53:lemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses
54:the notation
55:$\max\{a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ and $\min\{a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ for finite sets of
56:**real** numbers from this page onwards.
57:
58:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-nat-add-associative.md
36:## Statement
37:
38:For all $a, b, c \in \mathbb{N}$: $(a + b) + c = a + (b + c)$.
39:
40:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-nat-add-commutative.md
36:## Statement
37:
38:For all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$: $m + n = n + m$.
39:
40:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-nat-order-add-compatible.md
34:## Statement
35:
36:For all $m, n, k \in \mathbb{N}$: $m \le n \iff m + k \le n + k$; and $m < n \iff m + k < n + k$ ([[def-nat-order]]).
37:
38:## Facts & Assumptions

