    17	
    18	**Objective.** This page is where the least-upper-bound property first produces
    19	**limits**. Everything on the sequences page, linked under Prerequisites above,
    20	held in any Archimedean ordered field: uniqueness of limits, the algebra of
    21	limits, the order limit theorem and the squeeze theorem are all statements whose
    22	proofs never open the completeness axiom. Nothing there could produce a limit
    23	that was not handed to it. The four results proved here all can, and each of them
    24	spends completeness exactly once: the monotone convergence theorem, the nested
    25	interval property, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and the Cauchy criterion.
    26	
    27	The order of the page is chosen so that the use of completeness stays in one
    28	place. [[thm-monotone-convergence]] is the only item that reaches for a supremum
    29	directly, and every later existence statement is routed through it. The nested
    30	interval property is monotone convergence applied to the two endpoint sequences.
    31	Bolzano-Weierstrass is [[lem-peak-monotone-subsequence]], which uses nothing
    32	about $\mathbb{R}$ beyond trichotomy of its order, followed by
    33	[[cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded]], which is where the axiom is actually
    34	spent. And [[thm-cauchy-criterion-via-lub]] is three cited lemmas in a row, two
    35	of which hold in any ordered field. A reader who wants to know where
    36	completeness is used on this page has one item to look at.
    37	
    38	**Monotone sequences.** [[def-monotone-sequence]] fixes the vocabulary, with the
    39	unqualified word *increasing* reserved for the strict notion, and settles one
    40	point that recursive constructions make constantly: comparing consecutive terms
    41	suffices, and the equivalence with the global condition is an induction, written
    42	out there rather than assumed. [[thm-monotone-convergence]] then says that a
    43	bounded monotone sequence converges, and names the limit: the supremum of the
    44	range in the nondecreasing case, the infimum in the nonincreasing one. That
    45	identification, not the bare existence, is what makes the theorem a tool for
    46	*computing* limits, as the recursive sequences of the companion page show.
    47	[[lem-monotone-unbounded-diverges]] closes the dichotomy: a nondecreasing
    48	sequence either converges to the supremum of its range or diverges to $+\infty$,
    49	with nothing in between, and [[cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded]] states the
    50	resulting equivalence in the form the rest of the page consumes.
    51	
    52	**Intervals and nesting.** [[def-interval]] records the nine order-convex forms,
    53	the nondegeneracy conditions, and length, with the deliberate warning that
    54	$\pm\infty$ is notation and never an element of $\mathbb{R}$. Its consumer is
    55	[[thm-nested-interval-property]], which is stated here in a slightly stronger
    56	form than usual: a nested sequence of nonempty closed bounded intervals
    57	intersects in $[\,\sup a_k, \inf b_k\,]$, not merely in something nonempty, and
    58	the intersection is a single point exactly when the lengths tend to $0$. Both
    59	hypotheses are load bearing and each fails on its own witness, one on each side
    60	of the companion page: dropping closedness gives the empty intersection of
    61	[[cex-nested-open-intervals-empty]], and dropping boundedness gives the empty
    62	intersection of [[cex-nested-unbounded-closed-empty]]. The proof needs no
    63	Archimedean input, which is worth saying because the textbook argument for the
    64	single-point case usually does use it; here the lengths converge to $b - a$ by
    65	the algebra of limits and the two directions are the two directions of
    66	$b - a = 0$.
    67	
    68	**Subsequences and Bolzano-Weierstrass.** [[def-subsequential-limit]] combines
    69	two notions already fixed on the sequences page, and stresses the distinction
    70	between a subsequential limit of a sequence and a limit point of a set. The work
    71	is in [[lem-peak-monotone-subsequence]], the peak or rising-sun lemma: **every**
    72	real sequence, with no hypothesis at all, has a monotone subsequence. Its two
    73	cases are literal negations of one another, so no completeness enters the split,
    74	and both recursions choose a *least* element supplied by the well-ordering
    75	principle, so no form of the axiom of choice is used either. That is the reason
    76	Bolzano-Weierstrass in $\mathbb{R}$ is choice-free, in contrast with sequential
    77	compactness in a general metric space. [[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]] is then two
    78	citations, and it is the exact repair of the false statement
    79	[[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]] proved on the previous page: boundedness does
    80	not give convergence, it gives a convergent subsequence.
    81	
    82	**Cauchy completeness from the axioms.** [[lem-cauchy-sequence-bounded]] and
    83	[[lem-cauchy-with-convergent-subsequence]] are the two ordered-field halves, and
    84	[[thm-cauchy-criterion-via-lub]] joins them to Bolzano-Weierstrass. This library
    85	already knew that every Cauchy sequence of reals converges: it is
    86	[[thm-reals-cauchy-complete]], proved on the Cauchy-construction page by a
    87	diagonal argument on representatives. The two proofs are not the same theorem
    88	twice. One is a fact about the object that construction produced; the other is a
    89	fact about the axioms, and holds in **every** complete ordered field however it
    90	was obtained. [[rem-completeness-routes]] sets out why both are kept, and states
    91	plainly which implications are *not* proved on this page: that Cauchy
    92	completeness plus the Archimedean property gives back the least-upper-bound
    93	property, and likewise for nested intervals. Those belong to the page on the
    94	equivalent forms of completeness, which comes later in the reading order, and no
    95	item here may be cited for them.
     1	---
