    31	
    32	## Statement
    33	
    34	Every Cauchy sequence of reals converges to a real ([[def-real-limit]]).
    35	
    36	More carefully, this is a statement about the *axioms*: in a complete ordered
    37	field, that is in an ordered field with the least-upper-bound property
    38	([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), every Cauchy sequence converges. The proof
    39	below uses nothing about $\mathbb{R}$ except that property, through
    40	[[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]].
    41	
    42	**This library already knows the conclusion by a different route.**
    43	It is proved on the Cauchy-construction page, where
    44	$\mathbb{R}$ is built out of Cauchy sequences of rationals and completeness is
    45	read off the construction. That proof is about a particular construction; this
    46	one is about the axioms, and it is what tells us the statement holds in *any*
    47	complete ordered field, however it was obtained.
    48	
    49	## Facts & Assumptions
    50	
    51	**Given:** A Cauchy sequence $(x_k)$ of reals, $\mathbb{R}$ being a complete ordered field.
    52	
    53	[L1] Every Cauchy sequence of reals is bounded ([[lem-cauchy-sequence-bounded]]).
    54	
    55	[L2] Bolzano-Weierstrass: every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent subsequence, that is a strictly increasing $n$ and a real $L$ with $x_{n_j} \to L$ ([[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]]).
    56	
    57	[L3] A Cauchy sequence with a subsequence converging to $L$ converges to $L$ ([[lem-cauchy-with-convergent-subsequence]]).
    58	
    59	[L4] Convergence of a sequence of reals to a real ([[def-real-limit]]).
    60	
    61	[L5] $\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field, and this is the only property of it used, through [L2] ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
    62	
    63	## Proof
    64	
    65	**Proof technique:** direct.
    66	
    67	1.1 The Cauchy sequence $(x_k)$ is bounded. [given, L1]
    68	
    69	2.1 Being bounded, $(x_k)$ has a convergent subsequence: fix a strictly increasing $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ and a real $L$ with $x_{n_j} \to L$. [step 1.1, L2, L5, choose]
    70	

