    14	---
    15	
    16	**Objective.** This page builds the working toolkit for limits of real
    17	sequences: the arithmetic of limits, the order properties of limits, the squeeze
    18	theorem, and the behaviour of limits under passage to a subsequence. Together
    19	with the suprema page it is what the rest of real analysis is written in.
    20	
    21	Convergence is **not** defined here. The published [[def-real-limit]] already
    22	says what it means for a sequence of reals to converge to a real and what it
    23	means to be Cauchy, exactly as [[def-complete-ordered-field]] already defined the
    24	supremum before the suprema page. What that definition leaves open is everything
    25	one computes with, and that is the content of this page. The first item,
    26	[[def-sequence]], adds only the surrounding vocabulary: bounded, eventually,
    27	frequently, tail, subsequence.
    28	
    29	One point of hygiene is settled at the outset. [[def-real-limit]] quantifies over
    30	**rational** $\varepsilon > 0$, because in the construction of $\mathbb{R}$ the
    31	rationals are available first. That loses nothing, since below any positive real
    32	lies a positive rational ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), and the argument is written
    33	out once in the remarks of [[def-sequence]]. Proofs here run the test against a
    34	real $\varepsilon$ wherever that is more convenient and cite that remark rather
    35	than switching silently.
    36	
    37	**The four load-bearing results.** [[lem-limit-unique]] is what licenses the
    38	notation $\lim_k x_k$ at all. [[thm-algebra-of-limits]] is the main theorem:
    39	limits respect sums, scalar multiples, differences, products and, under the
    40	right hypothesis, quotients. Its quotient case is where textbook proofs
    41	habitually cheat, since the estimate $|1/y_k - 1/y| = |y - y_k|/(|y_k||y|)$ is
    42	worthless without a lower bound on $|y_k|$; that bound, $|y_k| > |y|/2$
    43	eventually, is proved here from the reverse triangle inequality rather than
    44	waved through. [[thm-squeeze]] establishes convergence of a sequence that is not
    45	assumed to converge, which is what makes it a tool rather than a computation
    46	rule. [[lem-subsequence-inherits-limit]] gives the standard divergence test: two
    47	subsequences with different limits force divergence.
    48	
    49	**Where the older pages are consumed.** [[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]] is
    50	where round one's [[lem-finite-set-has-max]] does its main work: the first
    51	finitely many terms of a convergent sequence are bounded because a nonempty
    52	finite set of reals has a maximum, proved once by induction and cited here rather
    53	than re-derived. Two further items on this page take a maximum of finitely many
    54	reals and cite the same lemma. [[lem-index-map-grows]] is the corresponding
    55	consumer on the $\mathbb{N}$ side, turning a strictly increasing index map into
    56	the inequality $n_k \ge k$ by induction, using both that $0$ is the least natural
    57	and that $\mathbb{N}$ is discrete ([[lem-nat-discrete]]); neither ingredient may
    58	be dropped, as the remarks there show.
    59	
    60	**Divergence to infinity is divergence.** [[def-divergence-to-infinity]] fixes
    61	the meaning of $x_k \to +\infty$ and stresses what it does not mean: $+\infty$ is
    62	not a real number, no limit in the sense of [[def-real-limit]] is being claimed,
    63	and a sequence diverging to $+\infty$ is unbounded and therefore has no limit at
    64	all. The notation is an abbreviation and never an equation, in line with the
    65	library's refusal ([[rem-sup-conventions]]) to extend $\mathbb{R}$ silently.
    66	[[lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges]] then records the one clean bridge between the
    67	two notions, valid for sequences of positive terms.
    68	
    69	**The three false statements guard the three standard errors.** A bounded
    70	sequence need not converge ([[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]]); the true
    71	statement in that direction requires passing to a subsequence and is
    72	Bolzano-Weierstrass, which is not available at this point in the reading order.
    73	Strict
    74	inequalities are not preserved in
    75	the limit ([[fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality]]); only the non-strict form
    76	[[lem-limit-preserves-order]] survives, because a positive gap may shrink to
    77	nothing. And one convergent subsequence says nothing at all about the sequence
    78	([[fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence]]); the correct statement needs
    79	every subsequence, or a Cauchy hypothesis.
    80	
    81	**What is deliberately deferred.** Monotone sequences and the monotone
    82	convergence theorem, the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem, and the completeness of
    83	$\mathbb{R}$ in the Cauchy sense all belong to the next page of this track,
    84	*Monotone Sequences, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, where they
    85	are now proved ([[def-monotone-sequence]], [[thm-monotone-convergence]],
    86	[[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]]). None of them is available at this point in the
    87	reading order, and no item on this page may be cited for any of them. Cauchy completeness is the
    88	exception and is stated as such rather than left vague: every Cauchy sequence of
    89	reals converges, for the $\mathbb{R}$ this page works in, is already proved on the
    90	Cauchy-construction page ([[thm-reals-cauchy-complete]]); what the next page will
    91	add is a proof from the least-upper-bound property that does not go through a
    92	construction. This page itself proves only the easy half,
    93	[[lem-convergent-implies-cauchy]], and its remarks say exactly where the other
    94	half stands. Nothing here uses the least-upper-bound property
    95	except through the Archimedean property ([[thm-of-archimedean]]). The natural
    96	conclusion, that the results here are really results about any Archimedean
    97	ordered field and so about $\mathbb{Q}$, is true of the *statements* and needs
    98	one qualification about the *proofs*, which is set out at the end of
    99	[[rem-sequence-conventions]]: two of the items cited on this page are stated for
   100	$\mathbb{R}$ or for complete ordered fields specifically, so transferring an
   101	argument to $\mathbb{Q}$ means rerunning it with the $\mathbb{Q}$ counterparts,
   102	not citing these items unchanged.

