FILE items/def-abs-value.md
29:## Definition
30:
31:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). The **absolute value** of
32:$x \in F$ is
33:
34:$$|x| \;:=\; \begin{cases} x & \text{if } x \ge 0, \\ -x & \text{if } x < 0. \end{cases}$$
35:
36:## Remarks
FILE items/def-bounded-set.md
38:## Definition
39:
40:Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field
41:([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.
42:
43:The notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by
44:[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:
45:$u \in \mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$, and
46:$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:
47:
48:- $\ell \in \mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\ell \le s$ for all $s \in S$.
49:- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.
50:- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if
51:  there are $\ell, u \in \mathbb{R}$ with $\ell \le s \le u$ for every $s \in S$.
52:
53:## Remarks
FILE items/def-countable.md
43:## Definition
44:
45:Recall that a natural number is a von Neumann natural
46:([[def-natural-numbers]]): $0 = \varnothing$ and $\sigma(n) = n \cup \{n\}$, so
47:that
48:
49:$$n = \{\, m \in \mathbb{N} : m < n \,\} = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$$
50:
51:is itself the set of its predecessors. Here $<$ is the order of
52:[[def-nat-order]], which is defined additively, so the displayed identity is a
53:theorem and not a convention: it is [[lem-nat-order-is-membership]], proved
54:immediately above. Let $A$ be a set, and let $\approx$ be equinumerosity
55:([[def-equinumerous]]).
56:
57:- $A$ is **finite** if $A \approx n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
58:- $A$ is **countably infinite** if $A \approx \mathbb{N}$.
59:- $A$ is **at most countable** if it is finite or countably infinite.
60:- $A$ is **uncountable** if it is not at most countable.
61:
62:## Remarks
FILE items/def-divergence-to-infinity.md
43:## Definition
44:
45:Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]), with $\mathbb{R}$ ordered
46:as in [[def-real-order]] and [[def-complete-ordered-field]].
47:
48:- $(x_k)$ **diverges to $+\infty$**, written $x_k \to +\infty$, when for every
49:  $M \in \mathbb{R}$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $x_k > M$ for all
50:  $k \ge K$.
51:- $(x_k)$ **diverges to $-\infty$**, written $x_k \to -\infty$, when for every
52:  $M \in \mathbb{R}$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $x_k < M$ for all
53:  $k \ge K$.
54:
55:Equivalently, in the language of [[def-sequence]]: $x_k \to +\infty$ when the
56:property $x_k > M$ holds eventually, for every real $M$.
57:
58:## Remarks
FILE items/def-natural-numbers.md
31:## Definition
32:
33:The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**
34:([[def-inductive-set]]),
35:
36:$$\mathbb{N} = \omega := \bigcap\,\{\, I : I \text{ is inductive} \,\},$$
37:
38:which exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the
39:Axiom of Infinity supplies one inductive set to intersect within, and Separation
40:makes the intersection a set). On $\mathbb{N}$ we take
41:
42:$$0 := \varnothing, \qquad \sigma(n) := n \cup \{n\},$$
43:
44:the distinguished element and the successor function. Thus
45:$0 = \varnothing$, $1 = \{0\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, $3 = \{0, 1, 2\}$, and in general
46:$n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is the set of its predecessors.
47:
48:## Remarks
FILE items/def-real-numbers.md
28:## Definition
29:
30:The **real numbers** are the quotient ring
31:
32:$$\mathbb{R} := \mathcal{C} / \mathcal{N}$$
33:
34:of the ring of Cauchy sequences ([[thm-cauchy-ring]]) by the ideal of null
35:sequences ([[lem-null-ideal]]). The class of $(a_n)$ is written
36:$[(a_n)]$. Each rational $q$ maps to the class $\hat q$ of the constant
37:sequence $(q, q, q, \dots)$.
38:
39:## Remarks
FILE items/def-real-order.md
26:## Definition
27:
28:A real $x$ is **positive** when it has a representative $(a_n)$ together
29:with a rational $\delta > 0$ and an index $N$ such that $a_n > \delta$
30:for all $n \ge N$. Define
31:
32:$$x \le y \iff x = y \;\text{ or }\; y - x \text{ is positive},$$
33:
34:and $|x| = x$ if $x \ge 0$, $|x| = -x$ otherwise.
35:
36:## Remarks
FILE items/fs-bounded-implies-convergent.md
33:## Statement
34:
35:**False claim:** every bounded sequence of reals converges
36:([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
37:
38:The implication in the opposite direction is true and is
39:[[lem-convergent-implies-bounded]]: every convergent sequence is bounded. The
40:claim above asserts the converse. It is refuted by the alternating sequence
41:$(s_k)$ and the index map defined by $n_0 = 0$ and
42:$n_{\sigma(j)} = \sigma(\sigma(n_j))$: the refutation proves that $n$ is
43:strictly increasing, $s_{n_j} = 1$ for every $j$, and $(s_k)$ does not
44:converge.
45:
46:The sequence usually written $x_k = (-1)^k$ is introduced here by recursion
47:([[thm-recursion]]), as the unique $(s_k)$ with $s_0 = 1$ and
48:$s_{\sigma(k)} = -s_k$. That is the same sequence under a different presentation,
49:and it is chosen because the three inductions of the refutation read straight off
50:those two recursion equations, whereas the power notation would first have to be
51:unwound into them.
52:
53:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality.md
31:## Statement
32:
33:**False claim:** if $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ are convergent sequences of reals
34:([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]) with $x_k < y_k$ for every
35:$k \in \mathbb{N}$, then
36:
37:$$\lim_{k} x_k \;<\; \lim_{k} y_k .$$
38:
39:The correct statement replaces both strict inequalities by non-strict ones and is
40:[[lem-limit-preserves-order]]. The claim above is refuted by
41:$x_k = 0$ and $y_k = 1/(k+1)$, whose limits are both $0$.
42:
43:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-index-map-grows.md
44:## Statement
45:
46:Let $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ be a function, written $k \mapsto n_k$, and
47:recall that $n$ is **strictly increasing** when $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$
48:([[def-sequence]], [[def-nat-order]]).
49:
50:1. **Consecutive comparisons suffice.** If $n_i < n_{\sigma(i)}$ for every
51:   $i \in \mathbb{N}$, then $n$ is strictly increasing.
52:2. **Growth.** If $n$ is strictly increasing then $n_k \ge k$ for every
53:   $k \in \mathbb{N}$.
54:
55:Claim 1 is what one checks in practice when exhibiting a subsequence; claim 2 is
56:what every later subsequence argument uses.
57:
58:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-limit-preserves-order.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ be sequences of reals ([[def-sequence]]) converging to
45:$x$ and $y$ respectively ([[def-real-limit]]). If $x_k \le y_k$ eventually, that
46:is for all $k$ from some index on, then
47:
48:$$\lim_{k} x_k \;\le\; \lim_{k} y_k .$$
49:
50:In particular, if $x_k \ge 0$ eventually then $\lim_k x_k \ge 0$, and if
51:$a \le x_k \le b$ eventually then $a \le \lim_k x_k \le b$.
52:
53:The conclusion is **not** strict, and cannot be made strict; see the remarks
54:below and the false statement at the end of this page.
55:
56:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-pigeonhole.md
50:## Statement
51:
52:Let $\mathbb{N}$ be the von Neumann naturals, with $0 = \varnothing$ and
53:$\sigma(n) = n \cup \{n\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), and let $<$ be the order of
54:[[def-nat-order]], so that $m < n \iff m \in n$ and
55:$n = \{\, m \in \mathbb{N} : m < n \,\}$ ([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]).
56:Write $\approx$ for equinumerosity ([[def-equinumerous]]). Then:
57:
58:1. for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ there is **no injection** $\sigma(n) \to n$;
59:2. if $m < n$ then there is no injection $n \to m$;
60:3. if $n \approx m$ with $n, m \in \mathbb{N}$, then $n = m$;
61:4. $\mathbb{N} \not\approx n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
62:5. no natural number is equinumerous with a proper subset of itself: if
63:   $A \subseteq n$ and $n \approx A$, then $A = n$.
64:
65:Claim 1 is the pigeonhole principle in its sharpest form, that $n + 1$ pigeons do
66:not fit injectively into $n$ holes; the other four are the consequences the
67:library actually quotes. Claim 3 says a finite set is equinumerous with exactly
68:one natural number, so "the number of elements" is well defined. Claim 4 says
69:$\mathbb{N}$ is infinite. Claim 5 says no natural number is Dedekind-infinite.
70:
71:**Why this is proved here.** The next item on this page defines finiteness as
72:equinumerosity with a natural number, and the three size classes it introduces
73:are exhaustive by construction but mutually exclusive only because of claim 4.
74:Several later items also need claim 3 or claim 5. The principle is elementary and
75:belongs with the naturals, but it is about counting rather than about order, so
76:it is proved here, immediately before finiteness is defined, from induction and
77:the identification of the order with membership alone. Nothing below uses
78:ordinals, cardinals, or any later material.
79:
80:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-rat-archimedean.md
32:## Statement
33:
34:For every rational $x$ there is a natural number $n$ with $x < n$.
35:Consequently, for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a natural
36:$n \ge 1$ with $1/n < \varepsilon$.
37:
38:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals with $x_k > 0$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$
45:([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-order]]). Then
46:
47:$$(x_k) \text{ converges to } 0 \iff (1/x_k) \text{ diverges to } +\infty,$$
48:
49:with convergence as in [[def-real-limit]] and divergence to $+\infty$ as in
50:[[def-divergence-to-infinity]].
51:
52:The positivity hypothesis is essential and is not a convenience; see the remarks.
53:
54:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/lem-subset-of-countable.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $A$ be at most countable ([[def-countable]]) and let $B \subseteq A$. Then
45:$B$ is at most countable.
46:
47:The proof establishes the sharper statement about subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ from
48:which this follows: **a subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is finite if it is
49:bounded above, and countably infinite if it is not.**
50:
51:**No choice principle is used.** This is the point of the lemma rather than a
52:footnote to it. The enumeration of an unbounded $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is built
53:by always taking the *least* element of $S$ above the previous one, and the least
54:element of a nonempty set of naturals is canonical
55:([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]): it is determined by $S$, not selected from it.
56:Replacing "least" by "some" would turn the construction into an appeal to
57:dependent choice.
58:
59:## Facts & Assumptions

