FILE items/thm-algebra-of-limits.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ be sequences of reals ([[def-sequence]]) converging to
45:$x$ and $y$ respectively ([[def-real-limit]]), and let $c \in \mathbb{R}$. Then
46:
47:$$x_k + y_k \to x + y, \qquad c\,x_k \to c\,x, \qquad x_k - y_k \to x - y, \qquad x_k y_k \to x y,$$
48:
49:and if in addition $y \ne 0$ and $y_k \ne 0$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$, then
50:
51:$$\frac{1}{y_k} \to \frac{1}{y} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \frac{x_k}{y_k} \to \frac{x}{y}.$$
52:
53:The quotient case rests on an eventual lower bound for $|y_k|$, proved below
54:rather than assumed: $|y_k| > |y|/2$ for all sufficiently large $k$.
55:
56:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/thm-reals-cauchy-complete.md
34:## Statement
35:
36:Every Cauchy sequence of real numbers ([[def-real-limit]]) converges to a real
37:number. Together with [[thm-reals-ordered-field]], this completes the
38:construction: $\mathbb{R}$ is a complete totally ordered field.
39:
40:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/thm-squeeze.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(a_k)$, $(x_k)$ and $(b_k)$ be sequences of reals ([[def-sequence]]) with
45:
46:$$a_k \le x_k \le b_k \quad \text{eventually},$$
47:
48:and suppose $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ both converge to the same real $L$
49:([[def-real-limit]]). Then $(x_k)$ converges to $L$.
50:
51:The middle sequence is not assumed to converge; that is the point of the
52:theorem, and it is why the squeeze is a tool for *establishing* convergence
53:rather than for computing a limit already known to exist.
54:
55:## Facts & Assumptions
FILE items/thm-uniqueness-complete-ordered-field.md
36:## Statement
37:
38:Any two complete ordered fields $F$ and $G$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) are
39:isomorphic via a **unique** ordered-field isomorphism
40:([[def-ordered-field-isomorphism]]) $\varphi : F \to G$, and this $\varphi$
41:**fixes $\mathbb{Q}$** ($\varphi \circ \iota_F = \iota_G$). Consequently
42:$\mathbb{R}$ is the **unique complete ordered field up to a unique isomorphism**,
43:and it admits $\mathbb{Q}$ as an ordered subfield via $\iota_F$.
44:
45:## Facts & Assumptions

