    37	---
    38	
    39	Many texts, especially in measure theory, lattice theory and optimisation, work
    40	in the **extended real line** $\overline{\mathbb{R}} = \mathbb{R} \cup \{-\infty, +\infty\}$
    41	and adopt the conventions
    42	
    43	$$\sup \emptyset = -\infty, \qquad \inf \emptyset = +\infty, \qquad \sup S = +\infty \ \text{ for } S \text{ not bounded above}, \qquad \inf S = -\infty \ \text{ for } S \text{ not bounded below}.$$
    44	
    45	With those conventions in force, every subset of $\mathbb{R}$ has a supremum and
    46	an infimum in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$, and the two exceptional cases recorded in
    47	[[fs-every-set-has-sup]] disappear. The conventions are consistent and often
    48	convenient. The value $\sup \emptyset = -\infty$ is not arbitrary: it is forced
    49	by monotonicity under inclusion together with $\sup \{x\} = x$, since
    50	$\emptyset \subseteq \{x\}$ then gives $\sup \emptyset \le x$ for **every** real
    51	$x$, and $-\infty$ is the only element of $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ below every
    52	real. (Monotonicity together with $\sup \mathbb{R} = +\infty$ forces nothing
    53	here: it only gives $\sup \emptyset \le +\infty$.) The convention also makes
    54	$\sup(S \cup T) = \max\{\sup S, \sup T\}$ hold without side conditions.
    55	
    56	**This library does not adopt them.** Two reasons, both about keeping the
    57	foundations honest.
    58	
    59	- $\pm\infty$ are not elements of $\mathbb{R}$. The library's $\mathbb{R}$ is the
    60	  complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), and
    61	  $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ is not a field: $+\infty$ has no additive inverse, and
    62	  the expressions $(+\infty) + (-\infty)$ and $0 \cdot (+\infty)$ have no
    63	  definition that keeps the field axioms. Writing $\sup S = +\infty$ silently
    64	  moves the discussion into a different structure, and every subsequent algebraic
    65	  step then needs its own justification.
    66	- Suppressed hypotheses become invisible errors. If $\sup S$ is always defined,
    67	  a statement such as "$\sup(S + T) = \sup S + \sup T$" appears to be
    68	  unconditional, and the cases where it degenerates are hidden inside the
    69	  arithmetic of $\pm\infty$ rather than shown in the hypotheses.
    70	
    71	Accordingly, in this library:
    72	
    73	- $\sup S$ and $\inf S$ denote **real numbers**, and the notation is used only
    74	  after existence has been established. Existence comes from the
    75	  least-upper-bound property ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and its dual
    76	  ([[thm-infimum-property]]), each of which requires the set to be nonempty and
    77	  bounded on the relevant side.
    78	- No supremum or infimum is written down before its existence has been
    79	  established, and every statement that establishes existence says explicitly
    80	  what it assumes. The nonempty and bounded clauses in [[lem-sup-epsilon]],
    81	  [[lem-inf-epsilon]], [[lem-sup-monotone]], [[lem-sup-translate]],
    82	  [[lem-sup-scale]] and [[lem-sup-sum]] are load bearing, not decoration. Where
    83	  some other hypothesis does that work it is named instead of being suppressed:
    84	  [[lem-max-is-sup]] obtains existence from a maximum rather than from a
    85	  boundedness clause, and [[lem-sup-unique]], which asserts no existence at all,
    86	  needs neither clause.
    87	- $\sup \emptyset$ and $\inf \emptyset$ are simply undefined
    88	  ([[def-infimum]], [[fs-every-set-has-sup]]).
    89	
    90	A reader coming from a source that uses the extended-real conventions should
    91	therefore expect the statements here to look more heavily qualified than the ones
    92	they are used to. The mathematics is the same; the difference is where the case
    93	analysis is written down. The extended real line is introduced explicitly in
    94	[[def-extended-reals]] for later limsup and liminf arguments, with its order and
    95	partial arithmetic kept separate from $\mathbb{R}$.

