items/thm-limsup-subadditive.md:87:2.3 In the third situation, with $\Lambda = -\infty$, first note that there is a real $B$ with $y_k < B$ eventually: if $M$ is real, [L4] with $\varepsilon = 1$ gives $y_k < M + 1$ eventually, so $B := M + 1$ serves; and if $M = -\infty$ then $y_k \to -\infty$ by [L5], so $y_k < 0$ eventually and $B := 0$ serves. Also $\Lambda = -\infty$ gives $x_k \to -\infty$ by [L5]. Now let $c$ be an arbitrary real: since $c - B$ is real, $x_k < c - B$ eventually, and beyond the larger threshold both that and $y_k < B$ hold, so $x_k + y_k < (c - B) + B = c$ there. As $c$ was arbitrary, $x_k + y_k \to -\infty$, hence $\limsup_k(x_k + y_k) = -\infty = \Lambda + M$ by [L5] and the addition table. [step 1.1, L3, L4, L5, L7, L9]
