    34	## Statement
    35	
    36	Every Cauchy sequence of real numbers ([[def-real-limit]]) converges to a real
    37	number. Together with [[thm-reals-ordered-field]], this completes the
    38	construction: $\mathbb{R}$ is a complete totally ordered field.
    39	
    40	## Facts & Assumptions
    41	
    42	**Given:** A Cauchy sequence $(x_k)_{k \ge 1}$ of reals.
    43	
    44	[L1] Rational approximation: for any real $z$ and rational $\eta > 0$ there is $q$ with $|z - \hat q| < \hat\eta$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]).
    45	
    46	[L2] Archimedean property: for rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $k$ with $1/k < \varepsilon$ ([[lem-rat-archimedean]]).
    47	
    48	[L3] Cauchy definitions in $\mathbb{Q}$ and $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-rational-cauchy-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
    49	
    50	[L4] The embedding preserves and reflects order and arithmetic; triangle inequality in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]], [[thm-reals-ordered-field]], [[def-real-order]]).
    51	
    52	[L5] Reals are classes of rational Cauchy sequences ([[def-real-numbers]]).
    53	
    54	## Proof
    55	
    56	**Proof technique:** direct.
    57	
    58	1.1 For each $k \ge 1$ pick a rational $q_k$ with $|x_k - \hat q_k| < \widehat{1/k}$. [L1, choose]
    59	
    60	2.1 $(q_k)$ is Cauchy in $\mathbb{Q}$: given rational $\varepsilon > 0$, pick $k_0$ with $1/k_0 < \varepsilon/3$ and $K$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \widehat{\varepsilon/3}$ for $k, l \ge K$; then for $k, l \ge \max(k_0, K)$, $\widehat{|q_k - q_l|} \le |\hat q_k - x_k| + |x_k - x_l| + |x_l - \hat q_l| < \widehat{1/k} + \widehat{\varepsilon/3} + \widehat{1/l} \le 3\,\widehat{\varepsilon/3} = \hat\varepsilon$, and the embedding reflects order, so $|q_k - q_l| < \varepsilon$. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L4]
    61	
    62	3.1 Set $x := [(q_k)] \in \mathbb{R}$, the class of this rational Cauchy sequence. [step 2.1, L5]
    63	
    64	4.1 $x_k \to x$: given rational $\varepsilon > 0$, pick $k_1$ with $1/k_1 < \varepsilon/3$ and $K_2$ with $|q_k - q_l| < \varepsilon/3$ for $k, l \ge K_2$; for $k \ge \max(k_1, K_2)$, the difference $\hat q_k - x$ has representative $(q_k - q_l)_l$, whose absolute values $|q_k - q_l|$ are eventually below $\varepsilon/3$, so $|\hat q_k - x| \le \widehat{\varepsilon/3}$, and $|x_k - x| \le |x_k - \hat q_k| + |\hat q_k - x| < \widehat{1/k} + \widehat{\varepsilon/3} \le 2\,\widehat{\varepsilon/3} < \hat\varepsilon$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, L4]
    65	
    66	5.1 Every Cauchy sequence of reals converges in $\mathbb{R}$: the reals are complete. [step 4.1] ∎

