   138	**"Diverges" means "does not converge".** Divergence is the plain negation of
   139	convergence, so an oscillating bounded sequence diverges. Divergence *to*
   140	$+\infty$ or $-\infty$ ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]) is a strictly stronger
   141	and entirely separate condition, and the arrow in $x_k \to +\infty$ is an
   142	abbreviation, not an equation: this library never writes $\lim_k x_k = +\infty$,
   143	because $+\infty$ is not a real number.
   144	
   145	**What is deliberately absent from this page.** Monotone sequences, the monotone
   146	convergence theorem, the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem and the completeness of
   147	$\mathbb{R}$ in the Cauchy sense are none of them treated here. They are the
   148	subject of the next page of this track, *Monotone Sequences,
   149	Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, which comes later in the reading
   150	order; nothing on this page may be taken to have proved any of
   151	them. The last of the four is the one exception worth stating: for the
   152	$\mathbb{R}$ this page works in, every Cauchy sequence of reals converges to a
   153	real is already proved elsewhere in the library, by
   154	[[thm-reals-cauchy-complete]] on the Cauchy-construction page, and
   155	[[lem-convergent-implies-cauchy]] records where that leaves the two directions.
   156	The monotone convergence theorem and the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem have no such
   157	exception: neither is proved anywhere in this library as things stand.
   158	**How far this page is really a page about $\mathbb{R}$.** It assumes only that
   159	$\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field, and it uses completeness solely through
   160	the Archimedean property. It is tempting to conclude that every result here holds
   161	verbatim for sequences of rationals, and the earlier version of this remark said
   162	exactly that. The claim needs splitting in two, because it is true of the
   163	statements and not literally true of the proofs.
   164	
   165	The statements do transfer. $\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field
   166	([[thm-rat-ordered-field]]) and is Archimedean ([[lem-rat-archimedean]]), and the
   167	absolute value, the triangle inequality and the order arithmetic these proofs run
   168	on are established for an arbitrary ordered field, not for $\mathbb{R}$ in
   169	particular ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[lem-of-triangle-inequality]],
   170	[[def-ordered-field]]).
   171	
