    32	## Statement
    33	
    34	Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]) and let
    35	$S = \{x_k : k \in \mathbb{N}\}$ be its range.
    36	
    37	1. If $(x_k)$ is nondecreasing ([[def-monotone-sequence]]) and $S$ is bounded
    38	   above ([[def-bounded-set]]), then $(x_k)$ converges ([[def-real-limit]]) and
    39	   $$\lim_{k} x_k = \sup S .$$
    40	2. If $(x_k)$ is nonincreasing and $S$ is bounded below, then $(x_k)$ converges
    41	   and $$\lim_{k} x_k = \inf S .$$
    42	
    43	Both suprema and infima exist under the stated hypotheses: $S$ is nonempty, so
    44	the least-upper-bound property ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) supplies the
    45	first and [[thm-infimum-property]] the second, and each is unique
    46	([[lem-sup-unique]]).

